    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Fragile-X Syndrome (FXS) is the best known monogenic disease in the autism spectrum disorders. FXS occurs when the Fmr1 gene is epigenetically suppressed and the Fmr1-encoding protein (FMRP) is not produced. Previous work has shown that hippocampus-dependent spatial memory is impaired in both human patients and FXS mice. Memories are believed to be stored in engrams, which are a population of learning-activated neurons that are preferentially reactivated during memory recall. However, it remains unclear whether it is the lack of FMRP during embryonic development or the lack of sustained FMRP expression during adult memory formation that underlies the deficits observed in FXS. The objective of the present project is to determine whether deletion of FMRP in adult mice during a hippocampus-dependent contextual fear conditioning task affects subsequent memory recall. Using a virally-introduced recombinase, FMRP was selectively deleted in learning-activated hippocampal CA1 neurons of Fmr1 conditional knockout mice after the learning task but before the memory recall assay. Behavioral analysis showed that contextual memory recall is marginally impaired by this selective deletion strategy of FMRP. Histological analysis indicated that the loss of FMRP specifically in learning-activated neurons does not affect the efficiency of their reactivation during memory recall. The results suggest that the aforementioned selective FMRP deletion does not fully recapitulate the behavioral phenotype, and that engram reactivation during memory recall is not affected. Follow-up experiments aim to probe the putative role of FMRP in memory consolidation, a process that occurs upstream of the memory recall assay in the present study. Broadly, elucidating the mechanistic roles of FMRP in learning and memory advances the current understanding of the pathophysiology of FXS and may help identify new therapeutic mechanisms for the condition.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
